    <custom_item>
      type        : FILE_CONTENT_CHECK
      description : "1.189 OL09-00-001060"
      info        : "OL 9 pam_unix.so module must be configured in the password-auth file to use a FIPS 140-3 approved cryptographic hashing algorithm for system authentication.

GROUP ID: V-271624
RULE ID: SV-271624r1091584

Unapproved mechanisms that are used for authentication to the cryptographic module are not verified and therefore, cannot be relied upon to provide confidentiality or integrity, and DOD data may be compromised.

OL 9 systems using encryption are required to use FIPS-compliant mechanisms for authenticating to cryptographic modules.

FIPS 140-3 is the current standard for validating that mechanisms used to access cryptographic modules use authentication that meets DOD requirements. This allows for Security Levels 1, 2, 3, or 4 for use on a general-purpose computing system."
      solution    : "Configure OL 9 to use a FIPS 140-3 approved cryptographic hashing algorithm for system authentication.

Edit/modify the following line in the \"/etc/pam.d/password-auth\" file to include the sha512 option for pam_unix.so:

password sufficient pam_unix.so sha512"
      reference   : "800-171|3.5.2,800-171r3|03.05.07,800-53|IA-5(1),800-53r5|IA-5(1),CCI|CCI-004062,CSF|PR.AC-1,CSF2.0|PR.AA-01,CSF2.0|PR.AA-03,GDPR|32.1.b,HIPAA|164.306(a)(1),HIPAA|164.312(a)(2)(i),HIPAA|164.312(d),ISO-27001-2022|A.5.16,ISO-27001-2022|A.5.17,ITSG-33|IA-5(1),LEVEL|2A,NESA|T5.2.3,QCSC-v1|5.2.2,QCSC-v1|13.2,Rule-ID|SV-271624r1091584_rule,STIG-ID|OL09-00-001060,SWIFT-CSCv1|4.1,Vuln-ID|V-271624"
      see_also    : "https://workbench.cisecurity.org/benchmarks/27024"
      file        : "/etc/pam.d/password-auth"
      regex       : "^[\\s]*password([^#\\n\\r]+)pam_unix\\.so"
      expect      : "^[\\s]*password([^#\\n\\r]+)pam_unix\\.so([^#\\n\\r]+)(?i)@PASSWORD_ENCRYPTION@(?-i)"
    </custom_item>

    <if>
      <condition auto:"FAILED" type:"AND">
        <custom_item>
          type            : FILE_CONTENT_CHECK
          description     : "install bluetooth /bin/false"
          file            : "/etc/modprobe.d/* /etc/modprobe.conf"
          regex           : "^[\\s]*install[\\s]+bluetooth"
          expect          : "^[\\s]*install[\\s]+bluetooth[\\s]+/bin/false[\\s]*$"
          required        : NO
          min_occurrences : "1"
        </custom_item>

        <custom_item>
          type            : FILE_CONTENT_CHECK
          description     : "blacklist bluetooth"
          file            : "/etc/modprobe.d/* /etc/modprobe.conf"
          regex           : "^[\\s]*blacklist[\\s]+bluetooth"
          expect          : "^[\\s]*blacklist[\\s]+bluetooth[\\s]*$"
          required        : NO
          min_occurrences : "1"
        </custom_item>
      </condition>

      <then>
        <report type:"PASSED">
          description : "1.19 OL09-00-000046"
          info        : "OL 9 Bluetooth must be disabled.

GROUP ID: V-271449
RULE ID: SV-271449r1091059

This requirement applies to wireless peripheral technologies (e.g., wireless mice, keyboards, displays, etc.) used with OL 9 systems. Wireless peripherals (e.g., Wi-Fi/Bluetooth/IR keyboards, mice and pointing devices, and near field communications [NFC]) present a unique challenge by creating an open, unsecured port on a computer. Wireless peripherals must meet DOD requirements for wireless data transmission and be approved for use by the authorizing official (AO). Even though some wireless peripherals, such as mice and pointing devices, do not ordinarily carry information that need to be protected, modification of communications with these wireless peripherals may be used to compromise the OL 9 operating system.

Satisfies: SRG-OS-000095-GPOS-00049, SRG-OS-000300-GPOS-00118"
          solution    : "Configure OL 9 to disable the Bluetooth adapter when not in use.

Create or modify the \"/etc/modprobe.d/bluetooth.conf\" file with the following line:

install bluetooth /bin/false
blacklist bluetooth

Reboot the system for the settings to take effect."
          reference   : "800-171|3.1.17,800-171|3.4.6,800-171|3.4.7,800-171r3|03.01.16,800-171r3|03.04.06,800-171r3|03.04.06a.,800-53|AC-18(1),800-53|CM-7,800-53|CM-7a.,800-53r5|AC-18(1),800-53r5|CM-7a.,CCI|CCI-000381,CCI|CCI-001443,CN-L3|7.1.3.5(c),CN-L3|8.1.4.4(a),CSF|PR.IP-1,CSF|PR.PT-3,CSF|PR.PT-4,CSF2.0|PR.AA-05,CSF2.0|PR.PS-01,GDPR|32.1.b,HIPAA|164.306(a)(1),HIPAA|164.312(a)(1),ISO-27001-2022|A.5.14,ISO-27001-2022|A.8.20,ITSG-33|AC-18(1),ITSG-33|CM-7,ITSG-33|CM-7a.,LEVEL|2A,NESA|T5.4.2,NIAv2|NS33,NIAv2|NS34,NIAv2|NS38,NIAv2|SS15a,PCI-DSSv3.2.1|2.2.1,PCI-DSSv3.2.1|2.2.2,PCI-DSSv4.0|2.2.3,QCSC-v1|3.2,QCSC-v1|5.2.1,Rule-ID|SV-271449r1091059_rule,STIG-ID|OL09-00-000046,SWIFT-CSCv1|2.3,Vuln-ID|V-271449"
          see_also    : "https://workbench.cisecurity.org/benchmarks/27024"
          show_output : YES
        </report>
      </then>
    </if>

    <custom_item>
      type        : FILE_CONTENT_CHECK
      description : "1.190 OL09-00-001065"
      info        : "OL 9 password-auth must be configured to use a sufficient number of hashing rounds.

GROUP ID: V-271625
RULE ID: SV-271625r1091587

Passwords need to be protected at all times, and encryption is the standard method for protecting passwords. If passwords are not encrypted, they can be plainly read (i.e., clear text) and easily compromised. Passwords that are encrypted with a weak algorithm are no more protected than if they are kept in plain text.

Using more hashing rounds makes password cracking attacks more difficult.

Satisfies: SRG-OS-000073-GPOS-00041, SRG-OS-000120-GPOS-00061"
      solution    : "Configure Oracle Linux 9 to use 100000 hashing rounds for hashing passwords.

Add or modify the following line in \"/etc/pam.d/password-auth\" and set \"rounds\" to \"100000\".

password sufficient pam_unix.so sha512 rounds=100000"
