    <custom_item>
      type        : CMD_EXEC
      description : "1.394 OL09-00-003002"
      info        : "OL 9 local interactive users must have a home directory assigned in the /etc/passwd file.

GROUP ID: V-271833
RULE ID: SV-271833r1092607

If local interactive users are not assigned a valid home directory, there is no place for the storage and control of files they should own."
      solution    : "Create and assign home directories to all local interactive users on OL 9 that currently do not have a home directory assigned."
      reference   : "800-171|3.4.2,800-171r3|03.04.02a.,800-53|CM-6b.,800-53r5|CM-6b.,CCI|CCI-000366,CN-L3|8.1.10.6(d),CSF|PR.IP-1,CSF2.0|DE.CM-09,CSF2.0|PR.PS-01,GDPR|32.1.b,HIPAA|164.306(a)(1),ISO-27001-2022|A.8.9,ITSG-33|CM-6b.,LEVEL|2A,NESA|T3.2.1,Rule-ID|SV-271833r1092607_rule,STIG-ID|OL09-00-003002,SWIFT-CSCv1|2.3,Vuln-ID|V-271833"
      see_also    : "https://workbench.cisecurity.org/benchmarks/27024"
      cmd         : "output=$(/bin/awk -F: '($3>=1000)&&($7 !~ /nologin/){print $1, $3, $6}' /etc/passwd | while read user uid home; do [ -d \"$home\" ] || /bin/echo \"Home directory missing for user: $user ($home)\"; done); /bin/echo $output | /bin/awk '{print} END {if (NF == 0) print \"pass\"; else print \"fail\"}'"
      expect      : "^pass$"
    </custom_item>

    <custom_item>
      type        : CMD_EXEC
      description : "1.395 OL09-00-003005"
      info        : "OL 9 interactive users must have a primary group that exists.

GROUP ID: V-271834
RULE ID: SV-271834r1092214

If a user is assigned the Group Identifier (GID) of a group that does not exist on the system, and a group with the GID is subsequently created, the user may have unintended rights to any files associated with the group."
      solution    : "Configure the system so that all GIDs are referenced in \"/etc/passwd\" are defined in \"/etc/group\".

Edit the file \"/etc/passwd\" and ensure that every user's GID is a valid GID."
      reference   : "800-171|3.5.1,800-171r3|03.05.01a.,800-53|IA-2,800-53r5|IA-2,CCI|CCI-000764,CN-L3|7.1.3.1(a),CN-L3|7.1.3.1(e),CN-L3|8.1.4.1(a),CN-L3|8.1.4.2(a),CN-L3|8.5.4.1(a),CSF|PR.AC-1,CSF2.0|PR.AA-01,CSF2.0|PR.AA-03,GDPR|32.1.b,HIPAA|164.306(a)(1),HIPAA|164.312(a)(2)(i),HIPAA|164.312(d),ISO-27001-2022|A.5.16,ITSG-33|IA-2,ITSG-33|IA-2a.,LEVEL|2A,NESA|T2.3.8,NESA|T5.3.1,NESA|T5.4.2,NESA|T5.5.1,NESA|T5.5.2,NESA|T5.5.3,NIAv2|AM2,NIAv2|AM8,NIAv2|AM14b,QCSC-v1|5.2.2,QCSC-v1|13.2,Rule-ID|SV-271834r1092214_rule,STIG-ID|OL09-00-003005,TBA-FIISB|35.1,TBA-FIISB|36.1,Vuln-ID|V-271834"
      see_also    : "https://workbench.cisecurity.org/benchmarks/27024"
      cmd         : "/sbin/pwck -qr | /bin/awk '{print} END {if (NR == 0) print \"pass\" ; else print \"fail\"}'"
      expect      : "^pass$"
    </custom_item>

    <item>
      description : "1.396 OL09-00-003006"
      info        : "OL 9 groups must have unique Group ID (GID).

GROUP ID: V-271835
RULE ID: SV-271835r1092217

To ensure accountability and prevent unauthenticated access, groups must be identified uniquely to prevent potential misuse and compromise of the system."
      solution    : "Edit the file \"/etc/group\" and provide each group that has a duplicate GID with a unique GID."
      reference   : "800-171|3.5.1,800-171r3|03.05.01a.,800-53|IA-2,800-53r5|IA-2,CCI|CCI-000764,CN-L3|7.1.3.1(a),CN-L3|7.1.3.1(e),CN-L3|8.1.4.1(a),CN-L3|8.1.4.2(a),CN-L3|8.5.4.1(a),CSF|PR.AC-1,CSF2.0|PR.AA-01,CSF2.0|PR.AA-03,GDPR|32.1.b,HIPAA|164.306(a)(1),HIPAA|164.312(a)(2)(i),HIPAA|164.312(d),ISO-27001-2022|A.5.16,ITSG-33|IA-2,ITSG-33|IA-2a.,LEVEL|2A,NESA|T2.3.8,NESA|T5.3.1,NESA|T5.4.2,NESA|T5.5.1,NESA|T5.5.2,NESA|T5.5.3,NIAv2|AM2,NIAv2|AM8,NIAv2|AM14b,QCSC-v1|5.2.2,QCSC-v1|13.2,Rule-ID|SV-271835r1092217_rule,STIG-ID|OL09-00-003006,TBA-FIISB|35.1,TBA-FIISB|36.1,Vuln-ID|V-271835"
      see_also    : "https://workbench.cisecurity.org/benchmarks/27024"
      name        : "group_duplicate_gid"
    </item>

    <if>
      <condition type:"OR">
        <custom_item>
          type        : CMD_EXEC
          description : "sestatus targeted"
          cmd         : "/sbin/sestatus"
          expect      : "(?i)^[\\s]*Loaded policy name:[\\s]+targeted$"
        </custom_item>

        <custom_item>
          type        : FILE_CONTENT_CHECK
          description : "pam_faillock.so"
          file        : "/etc/pam.d/system-auth"
          regex       : "^[\\s]*auth[^#\\n\\r]+pam_faillock\\.so[^#\\n\\r]+"
          expect      : "^[\\s]*auth[^#\\n\\r]+pam_faillock\\.so[^#\\n\\r]+"
        </custom_item>
      </condition>

      <then>
        <custom_item>
          type        : CMD_EXEC
          description : "1.397 OL09-00-003010"
          info        : "OL 9 must configure SELinux context type to allow the use of a nondefault faillock tally directory.

GROUP ID: V-271836
RULE ID: SV-271836r1092637

Not having the correct SELinux context on the faillock directory may lead to unauthorized access to the directory."
          solution    : "Configure OL 9 to allow the use of a nondefault faillock tally directory while SELinux enforces a targeted policy.

Create a nondefault faillock tally directory (if it does not already exist) with the following example:

$ sudo mkdir /var/log/faillock

Update the /etc/selinux/targeted/contexts/files/file_contexts.local with \"faillog_t\" context type for the nondefault faillock tally directory with the following command:

$ sudo semanage fcontext -a -t faillog_t \"/var/log/faillock(/.*)?\"

Update the context type of the nondefault faillock directory/subdirectories and files with the following command:

$ sudo restorecon -R -v /var/log/faillock"
          reference   : "800-171|3.1.8,800-171r3|03.01.08a.,800-53|AC-7a.,800-53r5|AC-7a.,CCI|CCI-000044,CN-L3|8.1.4.1(b),CSF2.0|PR.AA-03,GDPR|32.1.b,HIPAA|164.306(a)(1),ISO-27001-2022|A.8.5,ITSG-33|AC-7a.,LEVEL|2A,NESA|T5.5.1,NIAv2|AM24,PCI-DSSv3.2.1|8.1.6,PCI-DSSv4.0|8.3.4,Rule-ID|SV-271836r1092637_rule,STIG-ID|OL09-00-003010,TBA-FIISB|45.1.2,TBA-FIISB|45.2.1,TBA-FIISB|45.2.2,Vuln-ID|V-271836"
          see_also    : "https://workbench.cisecurity.org/benchmarks/27024"
          cmd         : "dir=$(/bin/awk '/^\\s*dir/ { print $3 }' /etc/security/faillock.conf); if [ -z \"$dir\" ]; then echo \"fail - dir directive not found in /etc/security/faillock.conf\"; else /bin/ls -Zd $dir; fi"
          expect      : "faillog_t"
        </custom_item>
      </then>

      <else>
        <report type:"PASSED">
          description : "1.397 OL09-00-003010"
          info        : "OL 9 must configure SELinux context type to allow the use of a nondefault faillock tally directory.

GROUP ID: V-271836
RULE ID: SV-271836r1092637

Not having the correct SELinux context on the faillock directory may lead to unauthorized access to the directory."
          solution    : "Configure OL 9 to allow the use of a nondefault faillock tally directory while SELinux enforces a targeted policy.

Create a nondefault faillock tally directory (if it does not already exist) with the following example:

$ sudo mkdir /var/log/faillock

Update the /etc/selinux/targeted/contexts/files/file_contexts.local with \"faillog_t\" context type for the nondefault faillock tally directory with the following command:

$ sudo semanage fcontext -a -t faillog_t \"/var/log/faillock(/.*)?\"

Update the context type of the nondefault faillock directory/subdirectories and files with the following command:

$ sudo restorecon -R -v /var/log/faillock"
          reference   : "800-171|3.1.8,800-171r3|03.01.08a.,800-53|AC-7a.,800-53r5|AC-7a.,CCI|CCI-000044,CN-L3|8.1.4.1(b),CSF2.0|PR.AA-03,GDPR|32.1.b,HIPAA|164.306(a)(1),ISO-27001-2022|A.8.5,ITSG-33|AC-7a.,LEVEL|2A,NESA|T5.5.1,NIAv2|AM24,PCI-DSSv3.2.1|8.1.6,PCI-DSSv4.0|8.3.4,Rule-ID|SV-271836r1092637_rule,STIG-ID|OL09-00-003010,TBA-FIISB|45.1.2,TBA-FIISB|45.2.1,TBA-FIISB|45.2.2,Vuln-ID|V-271836"
          see_also    : "https://workbench.cisecurity.org/benchmarks/27024"
        </report>
      </else>
